{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a significant figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century. He was a member of the House of Toulouse and ruled Tripoli from 1102 to 1112. His life and reign were marked by various challenges and conflicts, which he faced with a combination of diplomacy and military might.\n\n*Family and background*\nRaymond III was born into the noble House of Toulouse. His father, William-Jordan, was the Count of Tripoli, and his mother, Hodierna, was a descendant of the Counts of Barcelona. This lineage provided him with a strong foundation in political and military affairs, which would be crucial during his reign.\n\n*Reign*\nRaymond's reign was marked by several key events. Upon ascending to the throne, he inherited a region that was under significant threat from the neighbouring Muslim powers, particularly the Seljuk Turks. To counter these threats, he pursued a policy of military expansion and diplomacy. He formed alliances with other Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire, aiming to strengthen the defensive capabilities of the Crusader states. Additionally, Raymond worked to improve the infrastructure of Tripoli, focusing on the construction of fortifications to better protect the city and its surroundings.\n\n*Death and legacy*\nRaymond III died in 1112, leaving behind a complex legacy. His reign was characterized by a blend of military successes and political challenges. While he managed to secure some victories against the Muslim powers, his efforts to fully stabilize the region were hindered by internal disputes and external pressures. Despite these difficulties, his contributions to the Crusader states were significant, and he remains an important figure in the history of the region.\n\n*Later years and succession*\nAfter Raymond's death, his brother, Bertrand, succeeded him as Count of Tripoli. Bertrand continued many of his brother's policies, aiming to maintain the political and military stability that Raymond had worked to establish. The succession marked a transition period for Tripoli, as the new leadership sought to navigate the ongoing conflicts and challenges of the Crusader states.\n\n*Conclusion*\nRaymond III's life and reign were a testament to the complexities and challenges of the Crusader era. His efforts to secure and stabilize the region, despite the numerous obstacles, highlight the political and military skills that were necessary for survival in the turbulent medieval world. His legacy continues to be a significant part of the historical narrative of the Crusades and the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.600783348083496}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0003002431185450405}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0003967689990531653}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.11535075306892395}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.01649610325694084}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.577531944960356e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.152301259338856e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576214658096433e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3431432545185089}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.5174338817596436}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09981309622526169}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -1.2304047346115112}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.03229498863220215}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.11675009876489639}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.05209809169173241}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.47745198011398315}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.08637183159589767}, {"token": " states", "logprob": -0.37447699904441833}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.5284402370452881}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0004954302567057312}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.7044345736503601}, {"token": "12", "logprob": -0.0807085931301117}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.0009716795175336301}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -0.013179215602576733}, {"to